    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Cal Raleigh** stands out as our primary anchor with a 74.56 efficiency score. Facing Martin P\u00e9rez (4.73 xERA), **Cal Raleigh** benefits from a high-upside matchup against a vulnerable southpaw, with his spot in the two-hole providing the volume necessary to carry our power categories. Following closely, **Gavin Sheets** (59.95) and **Tyler Freeman** (59.49) offer strong value; **Gavin Sheets** draws a favorable park-adjusted look against Houser, while **Tyler Freeman** is a must-start due to his elite sprint speed and a high-efficiency matchup against Freddy Peralta, where the combination of the pitcher's slow delivery and park factors mitigates the risk of a tough strikeout-heavy arm. Conversely, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains in the lineup despite a sub-60 score, as his ability to steal bases against Ragans (60% SB success potential) is simply too valuable to fade even against a premier left-handed arm.\n\nThe rotation decision involving **Lawrence Butler** was the most nuanced of the morning. While **Lawrence Butler** posted a 48.97 against Wheeler, his massive 13% platoon advantage was tempered significantly by the elite 2.89 xERA of the opposing starter. He remains a high-variance play, but the benching of **Bryson Stott** was effectively made for us by his absence from the starting lineup, as he continues to struggle for consistent playing time. We continue to monitor the status of **Yainer Diaz** on the IL, but for tonight, the focus is on maintaining our SB ceiling; players like **Jeremiah Jackson** and **Tyler Freeman** represent our tactical hedge against the scarcity of the category, leveraging slow pitcher deliveries to manufacture runs in otherwise difficult matchups. \n\nRisk management for this evening centers on our exposure to elite arms. Facing Skenes, **Geraldo Perdomo** carries a 47.47 score, heavily penalized by the SP skill factor, though his leadoff role keeps him marginally viable for runs. We are essentially punting the ceiling in these high-xERA matchups, relying on the volume of our two-hole hitters\u2014**Cal Raleigh**, **Taylor Ward**, and **Jung Hoo Lee**\u2014to counteract the danger posed by the likes of Wheeler and Rodriguez. Ensure your game cap tracking is precise, as our bench depth of **Luis Arraez**, **Brendan Donovan**, **Gleyber Torres**, **Wenceel Perez**, and **Drew Gilbert** is ready to rotate in should the late-game lineup transitions reveal further platoon edges.",
